--TEST--
a test class whose constructor declares a parameter is reported as that test failing rather than pretended past, and a sibling class with a no-argument constructor still runs (`rule:testing/constructor-is-setup`)
--RUN--
test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:testing/every-test-is-its-own-isolate-and-the-constructor-is-setup
// `rule:testing/constructor-is-setup`: the runner constructs a test class with
// no arguments, so a constructor declaring one has nothing to be called with.
// That is a failure of the test rather than a refusal of the program, which is
// why this is a run case and not a reject one: the classes around it still run.
use Core\Test;

final class PreparedTest {
    private int $prepared;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->prepared = 7;
    }

    #[Test]
    public function itReadsWhatTheSetupPrepared(): void {
        Test::assertSame($this->prepared, 7);
    }
}

final class UnbuildableTest {
    private int $prepared;

    // The runner has no argument to pass here.
    public function constructor(int $prepared) {
        $this->prepared = $prepared;
    }

    #[Test]
    public function itIsReportedAsFailing(): void {
        Test::assertSame($this->prepared, 7);
    }
}
--EXPECTF--
  PreparedTest
    ✓ itReadsWhatTheSetupPrepared      %f ms
  UnbuildableTest
    ✗ itIsReportedAsFailing            %f ms
      `UnbuildableTest`'s constructor declares 1 parameter(s), and this runner supplies none: `rule:testing/constructor-is-setup` makes the constructor `setUp`, and § 8's fixtures fill the test method's own parameters

  1 failed, 1 passed, 0 skipped, 0 flaky in %d ms
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
